    "body": "Bracco plans to invest $230 million in its first U.S. manufacturing facility for injectable contrast media, selecting Sanford, North Carolina, for a project expected to create 200 jobs .\nWhat’s Related\nThe sterile manufacturing plant will add production in the United States, Bracco’s largest market, to a network that already includes facilities in Europe, Asia, and Canada . Contrast media are used in diagnostic imaging to help doctors see inside the body.\nBracco said the additional space will help meet growing demand and make supplies more reliable for U.S. customers. North Carolina officials said the plant is expected to shorten delivery times and reduce transportation costs.\nThe planned jobs span manufacturing, engineering, technical operations, quality, warehousing and administration.\nRELATED STORIES: Caterpillar Plans $1 Billion Manufacturing Push in North Carolina | Corvid Picks North Carolina for $95 Million Defense Plant Expansion\nFrom Milan to North Carolina\n“Producing injectable contrast media here in Sanford brings our supply closer to American hospitals and patients and makes it even more resilient ,” said Fulvio Renoldi Bracco, vice chairman and CEO of Bracco Imaging.\nThe Milan-based company cited North Carolina’s workforce and established life sciences industry as reasons for choosing Sanford. The Sanford Area Growth Alliance said the community competed with other domestic and international locations for the investment.\nAccording to the alliance, the facility is expected to be fully staffed by 2031. State officials said the new positions could generate more than $20.6 million in annual payroll for the region.\nState grant depends on hiring\nNorth Carolina approved a Job Development Investment Grant for Bracco Imaging Operations USA LLC that could reimburse the company up to $2,859,300 over 12 years. Payments depend on annual verification that Bracco has met its job creation and investment targets.\nThe state estimates the project could add $1.78 billion to North Carolina’s economy over the grant’s term. The agreement also calls for directing up to $317,700 to a state fund that helps rural communities pay for infrastructure improvements.\nProject partners include Central Carolina Community College, the North Carolina Community College System, state workforce officials, Lee County, the city of Sanford, and Duke Energy.",
    "body_sha256": "463fc1cd759fa502cb133ad88acbdacc5e596720b85847fd08bc08905dc94b3f",
